diff --git a/docker/Dockerfile.arm64 b/docker/Dockerfile.arm64
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1f8f408
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docker/Dockerfile.arm64
@@ -0,0 +1,29 @@
+FROM debian:bookworm
+
+# Native aarch64 build environment. No i386 packages are needed here.
+# The SLSsteam .so links libstdc++/libgcc statically on aarch64, so bookworm's
+# GCC 12 stays self-contained even if the Steam runtime ships an older
+# libstdc++. The static libraries use g++-9 automatically when it is installed.
+RUN apt-get -y update && apt-get -y install \
+	g++ \
+	libssl-dev \
+	libcurl4-openssl-dev \
+	libluajit-5.1-dev \
+	pkg-config \
+	make \
+	wget \
+	p7zip-full \
+	git \
+	cmake
+
+#Install dotnet-9.0 (arm64 mirrors are available for Debian 12)
+RUN wget "https://packages.microsoft.com/config/debian/12/packages-microsoft-prod.deb" -O packages-microsoft-prod.deb && \
+	dpkg -i packages-microsoft-prod.deb && \
+	rm packages-microsoft-prod.deb && \
+	apt-get -y update && \
+	apt-get install -y dotnet-sdk-9.0
+
+ENV PKG_CONFIG_PATH="/usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/pkgconfig"
+
+RUN useradd -m sls
+USER sls
diff --git a/docker/build-arm64.sh b/docker/build-arm64.sh
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..1ee03b5
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docker/build-arm64.sh
@@ -0,0 +1,32 @@
+#!/bin/bash
+
+DOCKER_HOST="sls-host-arm64"
+
+SCRIPT_DIR="$(dirname "$(realpath "$0")")"
+cd "$SCRIPT_DIR"
+
+if [ -z "$TRACE" ]; then
+	TRACE=0
+fi
+
+if [ -z "$DEBUG" ]; then
+	DEBUG=0
+fi
+
+if [ -z "$NATIVE" ]; then
+	NATIVE=0
+fi
+
+docker build --platform linux/arm64 -f Dockerfile.arm64 -t $DOCKER_HOST .
+docker run \
+	--name $DOCKER_HOST \
+	--platform linux/arm64 \
+	-e "TRACE=$TRACE" \
+	-e "DEBUG=$DEBUG" \
+	-e "NATIVE=$NATIVE" \
+	--user=sls \
+	--rm \
+	--mount=type=bind,source=../,target=/src \
+	--workdir=/src \
+	$DOCKER_HOST:latest \
+	make $@
diff --git a/docs/ARM64.md b/docs/ARM64.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..c2c7d54
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/ARM64.md
@@ -0,0 +1,278 @@
+# SLSsteam on ARM64 / AArch64
+
+This document covers the native aarch64 port of SLSsteam. It targets the
+`steamrtarm64/steamclient.so` shipped by Valve's `linuxarm64` Steam client, not
+the x86 client running under FEX/box64.
+
+Three clients were looked at, the pattern table targets the newest one:
+
+| Client | `steamclient.so` SHA-256 | Size | Build date |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| reference (manifest `1792618285`) | `6a0dc7ac66376b11fb91728ec2fdcf1ca3d062b5c33a6c24a3fd8038df333663` | 47238400 | `Wed Oct 21 21:10:52 UTC 2026` |
+| previous, `steamdeck_publicbeta` | `f5240dccf8ba658e744222e48b181656517a0d42bfc93d8dc5a46cd4067eeceb` | 47790160 | `Thu Oct 22 21:36:26 UTC 2026` |
+| current, `steamdeck_publicbeta` (manifest `1793056502`) | `c3aa29fab1cf522ad8473c78b59bfac8f33e46e5fdfb19e24b9ab515b6e9e9e9` | 47794256 | `Mon Oct 26 22:12:06 UTC 2026` |
+
+Only `c3aa29fa...` is listed as supported (`src/supportedhashes.hpp`, mirrored in
+`res/updates.yaml`). `f5240dcc...` is not: the patterns were rewritten for the
+current build and never rechecked against it. `6a0dc7ac...` is not either: most
+signatures do not match it and SLSsteam refuses to start there (see Pattern
+validation). The hash of the loaded `steamclient.so` is always logged
+(`steamclient.so hash is ...`), unknown ones log a warning with the supported
+list, independent of `SafeMode` / `WarnHashMissmatch`.
+
+Reference package: `bins_linuxarm64_linuxarm64.zip`, 110383070 bytes, SHA-256
+`a77faaf185f7b0681886d1c30e989d2cd1821cea421d41556e733b9d065b85a1`,
+build date `Wed Oct 21 21:10:52 UTC 2026`. The `steamui.so` in the same package
+has SHA-256 `d937fd95c05597eccc55ffd69c1d804ffe0777c4134a9223bbef67de80f7f619`.
+The device's `steamui.so` is `06f732b9cdba888368adb283feeaf5fa0423fb257d5b3c0f6dc0ae829c4f71b6`
+(39194744 bytes, same build date).
+
+As of 2026-10-26 the public `steam_client_linuxarm64` manifest is still
+`1792618285`; the beta-suffixed ARM64 manifest names return 404, and the client
+beta is selected through the installed client's `package/beta` file. Do not use
+`bins_steamrt_linuxarm64` for ARM64 validation: it has the same SHA-2 as the x86
+`bins_steamrt_ubuntu12` package. The native client comes from
+`bins_linuxarm64_linuxarm64`.
+
+## What is architecture specific
+
+- **Build**: aarch64 uses a native 64 bit build (`ARCH=aarch64`), its own static
+  libraries in `lib/arm64/`, and the `linux-arm64` .NET runtime identifier for
+  the helper tools.
+- **ELF**: the loader accepts `EM_AARCH64`, Windows ARM64 PE files, and reads
+  `SHT_RELA` relative relocations.
+- **VFTables**: aarch64 places most vtables in `.data.rel.ro.local`. Relative
+  addends live in `.rela.dyn`, so the decompiler reconstructs slot values from
+  the RELA table before analysing them.
+- **Decompiler**: aarch64 string references are built from `adrp`/`adr` plus
+  `add` chains. Function walking understands `b`, `bl`, `b.cond`, `cbz`,
+  `cbnz`, `tbz`, `tbnz`, `br`, `blr` and `ret`.
+- **Patterns**: every arm64 signature was extracted from the native client and
+  made unique inside `.text`. Each pattern walks back to its function prologue
+  with an aligned 4 byte scan.
+- **SDK layouts**: `CUtlBuffer`, `CNetPacket`, `CProtoBufMsgBase`,
+  `CServerPipe`, `DepotInfo_t` and `AppLicensesChanged_t` use their LP64
+  layouts. `CSteamEngine::getUser` reads the user map at `0x1290` (size at `0x12A0`);
+  every element is a `{key, CUser*}` pair, like on x86_64.
+- **Current pipe**: the native client has no stable `IClientUtils::m_PipeIndex`
+  offset. The IPC frame hook records the current `HSteamPipe` per thread, and
+  that value is used by the ticket and fake-app-id paths.
+- **Instruction cache**: patched functions and trampolines are flushed with
+  `__builtin___clear_cache` before they execute.
+- **Trampolines**: the arm64 hook path builds the trampoline first, relocates
+  its PC-relative instructions (`adrp`, `adr`, `b`, `bl`, `b.cond`, `cbz`,
+  `cbnz`, `tbz`, `tbnz`, literal loads), writes the jump back, and only then
+  patches the target. It uses one atomic 4-byte branch when the hook is within
+  +/-128 MB, otherwise a 16-byte absolute jump. A branch that targets the
+  displaced prologue is redirected into the trampoline. If an instruction
+  cannot be reached from the trampoline, the hook is aborted before the target
+  is touched.
+- **Unhooking**: the original bytes are saved at placement and restored
+  directly; the trampoline is never freed while Steam is running because a
+  thread can still be inside the hook function.
+- **Hook targets**: every built-in arm64 hook starts with a non-PC-relative
+  instruction (`stp`, `sub sp`, or `mov`), so the 4-byte branch path only
+  displaces one instruction. The 16-byte absolute path is a fallback for
+  built-in hooks farther than 128 MB. Runtime Lua hooks refuse that wide path
+  because it is not atomic while Steam threads are running.
+
+The following hooks are resolved on arm64. Addresses are listed for both known
+clients; the signature table in `src/patterns.cpp` is the `c3aa29fa...` column.
+
+| Hook | `c3aa29fa...` (Oct 26, current) | `f5240dcc...` (Oct 22) | `6a0dc7ac...` (Oct 21) |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| `CAPIJob::SendAndRecv` | `0x6144e0` | `0x614500` | `0x5ef6a0` |
+| `CAppDataCache::BParseResponseMessage` | `0x59f8d8` | `0x59f8f8` | `0x577a00` |
+| `CWebSocketConnection::BBuildAndAsyncSendFrame` | `0x1b2c330` | `0x1b2bb38` | `0x1c97eb8` |
+| `CSteamEngine::GetServerPipe` | `0xecf570` | `0xeced78` | `0xe5c3d8` |
+| `CSteamEngine::SetAppIdForCurrentPipe` | `0xcdc298` | `0xcdbc10` | `0xc65260` |
+| `CSteamEngine::ProcessIPCFrame` | `0xcd50c0` | `0xcd4a38` | `0xc5e400` |
+| `CUser::CheckAppOwnership` | `0xd562c0` | `0xd55ae0` | `0xce2a98` |
+| `CUser::GetSubscribedApps` | `0xd619e8` | `0xd61208` | `0xcee128` |
+| `CUser::PostCallback` | `0x5d3a20` | `0x5d3a40` | `0x5af120` |
+| `CUser::PostCallbackToAppId` | `0x5d3ce8` | `0x5d3d08` | `0x5af578` |
+| `CUser::SpawnGameId` | `0xd67628` | `0xd66e48` | `0xcf3db0` |
+| `CUser::UpdateAppOwnershipTicket` | `0xd62a40` | `0xd62260` | `0xcef180` |
+| `CUserAppManager::BuildDepotDependency` | `0x5a6980` | `0x5a69a0` | `0x5821c8` |
+| `CDepotDownloadMgr::ProcessDepotManifest` | `0x5a8ed0` | `0x5a8ef0` | — |
+| `CDepotDownloadMgr::BYldRequestDepotManifest` | `0x8a17a8` | `0x8a1690` | — |
+
+Layout changes between builds are small (functions move by `0x20` in the low
+region and by `0x688` / `0x7e0` further up), but they change every `adrp`, `bl`
+and page offset, plus some struct member offsets: `UpdateAppOwnershipTicket`
+loads a `CUser` member with `mov x2,#0x53d0` in the Oct 22 build and
+`mov x2,#0x53f8` in the Oct 26 build. The signatures therefore wildcard these
+instructions (see the comment above the arm64 table in `src/patterns.cpp`).
+
+`CUser::PostCallback` has an identical twin right next to it (`0x5d3828`, same
+body, only the final tail call differs). Its signature therefore reaches back
+`24` bytes into the end of the previous function (`offset` in `Pattern_t`).
+
+The two `CDepotDownloadMgr` signatures are resolved by their unique
+instruction bytes plus a prologue scan, not by the log strings. The
+`"%s/depotcache/%d_%llu.manifest"` string at `0x22ab708` is not referenced
+inside `ProcessDepotManifest` (the path is built by a helper); the
+`CDepotDownloadMgr::BYldRequestDepotManifest` log string at `0x22e2540` is not
+referenced inside `0x8a1690` either. Do not use either as an anchor.
+
+- `ProcessDepotManifest`: `0x5a8ed0..0x5a9348` (FDE size `0x478`), prologue
+  `FD 7B B8 A9 FD 03 00 91 F7 63 03 A9`.
+- `BYldRequestDepotManifest`: `0x8a17a8`, prologue
+  `FD 7B B4 A9 FD 03 00 91 F3 53 01 A9`.
+
+The strongest cross-check is `bl 0x8a17a8` at `0x5a9118` inside
+`ProcessDepotManifest`.
+
+`ProcessIPCFrame` and `SetAppIdForCurrentPipe` are virtual methods of
+`CSteamEngine`; in the Oct 26 client they live in the vtable at `0x2c94ea8`,
+slots 17 (`ProcessIPCFrame`) and 27 (`SetAppIdForCurrentPipe`), numbered from
+the first virtual function as `tools/steamrt-analyzer` does (the large per-pipe
+routine is slot 21). The Oct 22 notes used 97 / 101 / 107 for the same three
+functions; those numbers are 80 higher and were taken from a different base.
+That vtable is the cheapest cross-check when a new build lands.
+
+`GetServerPipe` is **not** virtual, so it is not in that vtable. It is the small
+lookup `CServerPipe* (CSteamEngine*, HSteamPipe)` (`0xecf570`, 0x60 bytes, no
+prologue): index `pipe & 0xFFFF` into the vector at `engine + 0x48` (size at
+`+0x68`, 16 byte entries), then the entry's `CServerPipe::pipeHandle` (`+0x10`)
+must equal `pipe`. It has 17 direct callers. Slot 101 (`0xcd8df0`, FDE size
+`0xa78`, signature `(this, pipe, bool)`) is a large per-pipe routine that walks
+the user map and the pipe's callbacks. An earlier revision of this port resolved
+`GetServerPipe` to it by mistake; calling it from the ConnectPipe path made
+`steam.real` segfault 13-15 seconds after start.
+
+## Pattern validation
+
+An address from a signature is only accepted when it is verified against the
+unwind table (`.eh_frame`) of `steamclient.so`:
+
+- the signature must match exactly once in `.text` (on arm64 an ambiguous
+  signature is an error instead of "use the last hit");
+- the resolved address must be the entry of a function, and that function's size
+  must be inside the `minSize`/`maxSize` window declared next to the pattern.
+
+- the address must be 4 byte aligned, inside `.text` of its module and start with
+  the declared prologue bytes;
+- every non optional pattern must declare its size window, a pattern without one
+  is rejected.
+
+If any check fails `Patterns::init()` logs the pattern name, address and the
+function found there, clears every resolved address and returns false. `load()`
+then stops before `Hooks::init()`, so **no hook is placed** and Steam runs
+without SLSsteam. This is what happens on the Oct 21 client (`6a0dc7ac...`): the
+signatures are those of `f5240dcc...`, so SLSsteam stays out instead of patching
+unrelated functions.
+
+Hooks are all or nothing as well. `Hooks::init()` first verifies every detour
+target (inside `.text`, aligned, entry of a function in the unwind table), then
+places them; if one cannot be placed everything placed so far is removed and
+`init()` returns false. The interface hooks placed later by
+`Hooks::placeVFTHooks()` roll back the same way.
+
+## Smoke test after a Steam update
+
+1. `python tools/steamrt-analyzer/verify_arm64_patterns.py steamclient.so`
+   (exit code = number of failing patterns).
+2. On the device (or from a PC with `--ssh user@host [-i key]`):
+   `tools/smoke-test.sh [--restart] [--minutes 5] [--strict]`. It checks
+   "Loaded successfully" in the current session of `~/.SLSsteam.log`, that no
+   pattern or hook was rejected, the hash, and that `steam.real` stays up without
+   a new coredump. `--restart` restarts Game Mode (do not use it while someone
+   else uses the device).
+3. When both pass, add the new hash to `src/supportedhashes.hpp` and
+   `res/updates.yaml`.
+
+`TraceIPC` is optional on arm64. It only feeds extended IPC tracing and has no
+stable signature, so a missing hook does not abort startup.
+
+## Building
+
+Native aarch64 Linux (recommended):
+
+```bash
+# 1. Build the static libraries once. GCC 9 is preferred for the Steam ABI.
+make libs-arm64
+
+# 2. Build SLSsteam and the helper binaries.
+make build
+
+# 3. Install the same way as on x86.
+make install
+```
+
+`libs-arm64` builds libmem 5.1.5 (bundled static: libmem + capstone + keystone +
+llvm), yaml-cpp 0.7.0, protobuf 3.15.3 and LuaJIT 2.1 into `lib/arm64/`. It uses
+`gcc-9`/`g++-9` when available and the host GCC otherwise, always with
+`-D_GLIBCXX_USE_CXX11_ABI=0`.
+
+SLSsteam itself needs **GCC 10 or newer** because the source uses C++20 library
+features (`std::unordered_map::contains`, `std::string::starts_with`, the chrono
+calendar). GCC 9 is only required for the static libraries whose ABI has to
+match Valve's runtime. On aarch64 the final `.so` links `libstdc++` and
+`libgcc` statically, so a newer host compiler is safe even when the Steam
+runtime ships an older libstdc++.
+
+Cross compilation from an x86_64 host works when an aarch64 sysroot and
+`aarch64-linux-gnu-g++` are installed:
+
+```bash
+make libs-arm64 CROSS=aarch64-linux-gnu-
+make build ARCH=aarch64 CXX=aarch64-linux-gnu-g++ PKG_CONFIG=aarch64-linux-gnu-pkg-config
+```
+
+Docker (aarch64 container):
+
+```bash
+cd docker
+./build-arm64.sh build
+```
+
+The arm64 container builds with Debian bookworm's GCC and relies on the static
+libstdc++ link. If you want Valve's exact GCC 9 ABI for the libraries, install
+`g++-9` in the image as well.
+
+## Validation performed
+
+- All 49 translation units compile for `aarch64-linux-gnu`.
+- The reference binary SHA-256 is
+  `6a0dc7ac66376b11fb91728ec2fdcf1ca3d062b5c33a6c24a3fd8038df333663`,
+  matching `docs/ARM64.md`, `src/update.cpp` and `res/updates.yaml`.
+- Every arm64 pattern matches exactly once in the reference `steamclient.so`.
+  The optional `TraceIPC`, `CSteamEngine::m_pUser` and
+  `IClientUtils::m_PipeIndex` signatures are intentionally empty.
+- Every pattern's aligned backwards prologue scan returns the intended function
+  entry.
+- `CSteamEngine::getUser`: the native ARM64 `CSteamEngine` loads the user map from
+  `engine + 0x1290` and its size from `engine + 0x12A0`, and indexes it with
+  `add x, base, index, lsl #4` / `ldr x, [x, #8]` (`0xccc2b8`, `0xcd1e18`, the
+  per-user loop of `0xcd8df0`), calling a `CUser` virtual (`[vtbl + 0x88]`) on
+  every value. That is the same `{key, CUser*}` element as x86_64, only at a
+  different base offset. The `0x11C0` vector (8-byte elements, size at `0x11D0`)
+  seen in `0x6b424c` belongs to other classes and is a plain pointer list.
+- The ARM64 client contains the type name `12CUserFriends`; `18CUserFriends`
+  is absent. The code selects the vftable by typeinfo name ending in
+  `CUserFriends`, so it does not depend on the current length prefix.
+- The arm64 decompiler's string-reference pass resolves every interface map name
+  used by `VFTIndexes` (`IClientUserMap`, `IClientUtilsMap`, `IClientCompatMap`,
+  `IClientConfigStoreMap`, `IClientFriendsMap`, `IClientRemoteStorageMap`).
+- `IClientAppsMap` and `IClientAppManagerMap` embed numeric log ids instead of
+  method names on both x86_64 and aarch64. Their fixed indices match the x86_64
+  client of the same version:
+  - Apps: `GetAppData=0`, `GetAppDataSection=5`, `RequestAppInfoUpdate=7`,
+    `GetDLCCount=8`, `BGetDLCDataByIndex=9`, `GetAppType=10`.
+  - AppManager: `InstallApp=0`, `UninstallApp=1`, `LaunchApp=2`,
+    `GetAppInstallState=4`, `IsAppDlcInstalled=9`, `BIsDlcEnabled=11`,
+    `GetUpdateInfo=20`, `GetAppStateInfo=71`, `BCanRemotePlayTogether=73`,
+    `GetNumLibraryFolders=75`, `GetLibraryFolderPath=76`,
+    `GetLibraryFolderLabel=79`.
+
+## Client updates
+
+A Steam client update can move any of the addresses above. If SLSsteam logs
+`Unable to find signature` / `Failed to resolve address for hook`, re-extract
+the patterns from the new `steamrtarm64/steamclient.so`:
+
+1. Find the function by its string anchor or its interface map index.
+2. Pick a 12-20 byte sequence inside the function that is unique in `.text`.
+3. Use the first 12 bytes of the function as the prologue vector.
+4. Run the smoke test, then add the SHA-256 to `src/supportedhashes.hpp` and `res/updates.yaml`.
diff --git a/docs/STEAMRT64.md b/docs/STEAMRT64.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..043b6e8
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/STEAMRT64.md
@@ -0,0 +1,171 @@
+# SLSsteam on the steamrt64 client
+
+Valve ships two Linux client lines. The legacy one is the 32 bit
+`ubuntu12_32/steamclient.so` that SLSsteam has hooked so far. The new one runs
+the client itself from `steamrt64/` under the rebuilt Steam Runtime and loads
+`steamrt64/steamclient.so`, an ELF64 x86_64 module. Until SLSsteam is built for
+x86_64 and its offsets/patterns come from that module, the new client runs
+without it.
+
+## How the client picks the runtime
+
+`steam.sh` (package `steam_ubuntu12`) contains the switch:
+
+```sh
+# Opt-in to the steamrt3c client
+case "${STEAM_FORCE_CLIENT-}" in
+	(ubuntu12_32 | scout | ldlp)
+		rm -f "$STEAMROOT/.steam-enable-steamrt64-client" ;;
+	(steamrt64 | steamrt3c | steamrt)
+		touch "$STEAMROOT/.steam-enable-steamrt64-client" ;;
+	("")
+		;;
+	(*)
+		log "Unknown value for STEAM_FORCE_CLIENT: $STEAM_FORCE_CLIENT" ;;
+esac
+
+if [ -e "$STEAMROOT/.steam-enable-steamrt64-client" ]; then
+	if [ -x "$STEAMROOT/steamrt64/steam" ]; then
+		unset STEAM_RUNTIME
+		unset STEAM_RUNTIME_LIBRARY_PATH
+		create_legacy_entry_points
+		"$STEAMROOT/steamrt64/steam" "$@"
+		STATUS=$?
+		...
+		exit $STATUS
+	fi
+fi
+```
+
+The marker is created by the launcher from `STEAM_FORCE_CLIENT`, and once it
+exists the launcher runs `steamrt64/steam` as a child, not via `exec`. The
+`LD_AUDIT` wrapper from `setup.sh` therefore still starts a process whose child
+inherits the environment, and the process name stays `steam`, so the guard in
+`setup()` keeps working. That inheritance is plausible but not proven here:
+`steamrt64/steam` embeds `pv-runtime/steam-runtime-steamrt`, and pressure-vessel
+is allowed to filter the environment. Treat re-injection under the new runtime
+as an open item until it is seen on a live client.
+
+## Reference build
+
+- client manifest: `1792618285`
+- package: `bins_steamrt_ubuntu12.zip` (`sha2`
+  `8c6c8605d5ab24577cae41f5b59e550d841026652eee8e65da27aee91cac20ed`)
+- `steamrt64/steamclient.so`: ELF64 x86_64, 52.8 MB, SHA-256
+  `17652d599bb46b6f316d59c37215efeb74ef892c8c3e7589df7d766ea3a9efa2`
+- `steamrt32/steamclient.so`: ELF32 x86, 55.4 MB, SHA-256
+  `1a7903dc673efea5684748303f9ff6d09df69473891cb93075e12c343639df24`
+
+Both hashes are in `res/updates.yaml` under the current release tag.
+
+The binaries can be fetched and inspected with `tools/steamrt-analyzer`.
+
+## What the port changes
+
+- **Architecture**: `ARCH=x86_64` build, `lib/x86_64` static libraries and the
+  `linux-x64` runtime identifier. `SLS_ARCH_64` in `src/arch.hpp` now marks the
+  LP64 targets.
+- **Decompiler**: the 32 bit x86 string reference pass relies on PIC thunks,
+  which x86_64 does not use. `src/decompiler.cpp` now reads RIP relative
+  operands for `SLS_ARCH_X86_64` and walks only the linear body, stopping at the
+  first `ret`, `jmp` or `int3`. Following branch targets was tried first and is
+  wrong here: interface wrappers tail call into neighbouring functions, so the
+  walk attributed their strings to the slot and produced tens of thousands of
+  bogus map entries. String collection now selects every `.rodata*` section of
+  the module by prefix instead of a fixed list, because GCC splits literals by
+  alignment (`.rodata.str1.8` is present in the new client).
+- **VFTables**: the interface maps whose methods only carry numeric log ids are
+  read from the fixed table on both 64 bit targets, like arm64 already did
+  (`SLS_VFT_INDEX`). Every other map resolves dynamically through the corrected
+  string pass. The table below is what the analyser reports for the reference
+  build and what the runtime is expected to produce.
+- **CSteamEngine user vector**: offset `0x11C0`, size at `0x11D0`. An element
+  spans two pointer fields and the `CUser*` is the second one, so the stride is
+  `2 * sizeof(void*)` and the pointer sits at `+8`. Confirmed in the reference
+  binary in `CSteamEngine`'s pipe walker:
+
+  ```
+  0x17c58a0: mov eax, dword ptr [r12 + 0x11d0]   ; size
+  0x17c58d0: mov rax, qword ptr [r12 + 0x11c0]   ; base
+  0x17c58da: mov rdi, qword ptr [rax + rbp + 8]  ; rbp = index * 0x10
+  ```
+
+  `src/sdk/CSteamEngine.cpp` keeps the arm64 branch as it was and uses this pair
+  layout for the other 64 bit targets. The two layouts really do differ: the
+  arm64 client stores plain 8 byte pointers (`ldr x1, [base, index, lsl #3]` at
+  `0x6b424c` in `steamrtarm64/steamclient.so`), while the x86_64 client stores
+  the 16 byte pair above. Do not share the indexing between them.
+- **Current pipe**: `0x1F18` shows up in the x86_64 client as dword
+  loads/stores, but the access shape near `0x1072c31-0x1072c4d` looks like a
+  counter, so it cannot be called `m_PipeIndex` without more work. The IPC frame
+  hook records the pipe per thread and `IClientUtils::getCurrentSteamPipe`
+  returns it for every 64 bit build. Outside `ProcessIPCFrame` that value is 0,
+  so callers must treat "no pipe" as a valid state instead of indexing
+  `g_processMap`.
+
+## Resolved interface indices (reference build)
+
+```
+IClientUserMap          BLoggedOn=4  GetSteamID=10  BUpdateAppOwnershipTicket=69
+                        GetLegacyCDKey=87  SetLegacyCDKey=88
+                        GetAppOwnershipTicketExtendedData=105
+                        IsUserSubscribedAppInTicket=118  GetEncryptedAppTicket=121
+IClientAppsMap          GetAppData=0  GetAppDataSection=5  RequestAppInfoUpdate=7
+                        GetDLCCount=8  BGetDLCDataByIndex=9  GetAppType=10
+IClientAppManagerMap    InstallApp=0  UninstallApp=1  LaunchApp=2  GetAppInstallState=4
+                        IsAppDlcInstalled=9  BIsDlcEnabled=11  GetUpdateInfo=20
+                        GetAppStateInfo=71  BCanRemotePlayTogether=73
+                        GetNumLibraryFolders=75  GetLibraryFolderPath=76
+                        GetLibraryFolderLabel=79
+IClientUtilsMap         GetOfflineMode=17  GetAppID=19
+IClientCompatMap        BIsCompatLayerEnabled=0  SpecifyCompatTool=1
+                        BIsCompatibilityToolEnabled=3  GetCompatToolName=4
+                        GetCompatToolDisplayName=6
+IClientConfigStoreMap   SetString=13
+IClientFriendsMap       GetFriendGamePlayed=23
+IClientRemoteStorageMap IsCloudEnabledForApp=24
+```
+
+## Known good function anchors (reference build)
+
+Found by locating the only code reference to a class or method name string and
+walking back to the `.eh_frame` FDE start. These are anchors for the pattern
+work, not patterns yet.
+
+| Function | String anchor | Containing function |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `CSteamEngine` pipe walker | `GetServerPipe( hSteamPipe )` | `0x17c56a0` |
+| `CSteamEngine` pipe helper | `GetServerPipe(hSteamPipe)` | `0x17ab900` |
+| `CUser::CheckAppOwnership` | `CheckAppOwnership` | `0x184eac0` |
+| `CUser::GetSubscribedApps` | `GetSubscribedApps` | `0x1844840` |
+| `CUserAppManager::BuildDepotDependency` | `BuildDepotDependency` | `0x1052dc0` |
+
+`CSteamEngine::ProcessIPCFrame`, `CSteamEngine::SetAppIdForCurrentPipe`,
+`CAPIJob::SendAndRecv`, `CUser::PostCallbackToAppId`, `CUser::SpawnGameId` and
+`CWebSocketConnection::BBuildAndAsyncSendFrame` have no unique name string in
+this build. They need call graph work from the anchors above, or from the
+`IClientUserMap` / `IClientAppManagerMap` slots.
+
+## Still open
+
+1. **x86_64 pattern set** in `src/patterns.cpp`. `CSteamEngine::GetServerPipe`
+   is still unresolved. In the 32 bit reference client the existing signature
+   resolves to `0x1bc4fe0`, a two argument `(this, HSteamPipe)` lookup over a
+   12 byte table at `[this+0x30]` with the count at `[this+0x4c]`. Neither
+   x86_64 candidate matches that: `0x17c56a0` is reachable only as vtable slot
+   21 of `12CSteamEngine` (its address sits at `0x30f2160`) and reads a third
+   value from `edx`; `0x17ab900` has exactly two call sites and both pass four
+   arguments `(engine, pipe, value, flag)`. A `(this, pipe)` call into either is
+   not safe, so the pattern and the `getServerPipe` wrapper must stay out until
+   the real signature is established.
+2. **x86_64 static libraries** (`lib/x86_64`): libmem, yaml-cpp 0.7.0,
+   protobuf 3.15.3, LuaJIT 2.1, plus the `_GLIBCXX_USE_CXX11_ABI` choice that
+   matches the new runtime toolchain. `make libs-arm64` is the template.
+3. **Packaging**: `pkg/slssteam/PKGBUILD` installs the x86_64 branch to
+   `/usr/lib32` with `lib32-*` dependencies, and `docker/Dockerfile` adds the
+   i386 architecture and `g++-multilib`. `setup.sh` itself has no 32 bit
+   assumptions and does not need a change.
+4. **Runtime validation** against a live `steamrt64/steam` client, including
+   whether the `LD_AUDIT` environment survives the launcher and pressure-vessel.
+   Nothing in this document was verified by running the client; every claim
+   comes from the shipped binaries.
